!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/orders-api-3.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/http-api-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/order-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/orders-errors-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  The orders service, described as types in an ordinary schema.

  FOUR IMPORTS, which is the shape of the thing: the API vocabulary, the domain types, the errors,
  and core. Until UPSTREAM.md #11 was fixed this had to be one file carrying all of it, because a
  schema could reference types from exactly one other schema. The split is what the fix bought, and
  it is the ordinary one -- domain types are reusable independent of the API that exposes them, and
  a second version of this API would import the same order-1.tn.

  HOW THE METADATA IS CARRIED. A FIXED field (§5.2's `= value`) puts a value in the type where an
  annotation would have put it beside the type. `method: http_method = POST` is checked by the
  resolver and survives into resolver output with its value, where a locally declared annotation's
  value is silently dropped (UPSTREAM.md #12).

  HOW A RESPONSE IS CARRIED. A response is one of several, which is what `choice` is for. Each
  variant fixes its own status, so a reader can see which status carries which body. The variants
  are NOT proved disjoint and need not be: §5.4 requires distinct variant types, not disjoint value
  sets, and record-set disjointness is a case a resolver may leave unproven. So a response value
  carries its tag -- !order_created { ... } -- which names the response rather than inferring it.

  WHAT AN OPERATION VALUE IS. Because an operation is an ordinary record, a value of one is an
  exchange: { method: POST  path: "/orders"  request: <an order>  response: !order_created { ... } }
  is one request and its reply. The same declaration is the contract and the shape of a trace.

  WHAT IT COSTS. Every response needs a named wrapper type -- five of them below -- whose only job
  is to pair a status with a body. In a meta layer those would be values, not declarations. That is
  the trade sketch/README.md sets out.
  """
{
  order_created  => { status: status_code = 201  body: order }
  order_invalid  => { status: status_code = 400  body: problem }
  order_sku_gone => { status: status_code = 404  body: sku_not_found }

  @doc:"Accept an order and confirm it."
  create_order => operation & {
    method:   http_method = POST
    path:     text = "/orders"
    request:  order
    response: (order_created | order_invalid | order_sku_gone)
  }

  schema_served  => { status: status_code = 200 }
  schema_missing => { status: status_code = 404  body: problem }

  @doc:"A record at a type position is not permitted (§5.2), so a parameter set is a named type."
  get_schema_parameters => { schema_path: non_empty_text }

  @doc:"Fetch a published schema. A schema document is not a TSON value, so schema_served has no body."
  get_schema => operation & {
    method:     http_method = GET
    path:       text = "/schemas/{schema_path}"
    parameters: get_schema_parameters
    response:   (schema_served | schema_missing)
  }
}
